WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:08.630
 You can buy mass produced
skis from a large company

00:00:08.630 --> 00:00:10.910
or you could choose to
purchase a handcrafted

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.890
said from a small independent.

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.780
It's a matter of
preference, much

00:00:14.780 --> 00:00:17.420
like drinking beer
produced by a major brewery

00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:19.070
versus a microbrewery.

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:23.000
In fact, many ski buying guides
call small independent brands

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:23.800
microbrews.

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:30.340
Handmade skis typically
feature exclusive designs

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:32.650
with high-end craftsmanship.

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.420
They're produced in
limited quantities

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.430
by small independent companies.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:42.280
This maker builds
the core of the ski

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:47.860
from two types of wood, Colorado
aspen and Colorado pine.

00:00:47.860 --> 00:00:50.350
They're harvested from trees
killed by pine beetles.

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:55.480
Workers glue together 8
to 10 pieces depending

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.220
on the width of the ski.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.230
The pieces form
a laminated block

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.860
that's structurally stronger
than a solid piece of wood.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.710
They clamp the block in
a press for two hours

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:06.595
while the glue dries.

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.560
After they remove the
block from the press,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.570
they use a band saw
to cut off strips.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.850
Each strip will be
the core of a ski.

00:01:16.850 --> 00:01:19.175
One block typically
makes six ski cores.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:25.720
Next, they shape the ski cores
with a router using a template

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:26.995
underneath the ski contour.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:36.200
They apply a blowtorch to strips
of ultra high molecular weight

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:37.575
plastic to make it bondable.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:42.500
Then they coat the plastic with
epoxy and adhere a strip of it

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.100
to each side of the ski core.

00:01:45.710 --> 00:01:48.020
This forms a sturdy
side wall to protect

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.370
the core from side impact.

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.830
After the epoxy sets, they
saw six inches off the front

00:01:54.830 --> 00:01:55.940
and back of the ski.

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.100
With a hand planer, they taper
the thickness of the ends.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.355
Then they heat the sidewalls
again to prep them for bond.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.860
But first, workers
glue a plastic core

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.590
called a tip spacer to the
front of the wood core.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:25.645
This holds the parts
together during construction.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.950
Then they use a hand crank dye
machine to dye cut the brand

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.470
name and logo into
a strip of ultra

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:35.955
high molecular weight plastic.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:43.550
They die cut the
same letters in white

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:46.850
then place them in the
black cutout strip.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.075
This is the ski's base.

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.240
Now they can begin
constructing the ski's.

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:55.580
They take a left and
right base and spread

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:57.275
epoxy over the entire surface.

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:03.080
They lay down a layer of
epoxy saturated fiberglass

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.210
and position the
ski core on top,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:09.480
then they spread epoxy on
the core and tip spacers

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:11.700
as well as lay extra
fiberglass where

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:13.140
the bindings will be installed.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.870
They position steel plates
for mounding the bindings,

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:22.320
then cover the entire ski with
another layer of fiberglass.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:27.620
Finally, they add
a nylon top sheet

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:28.870
printed with a graphic design.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:33.500
They cover the assembly with
a series of metal sheets

00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:36.770
and silicon heating blankets,
then place everything

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.420
in a custom designed ski press.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.700
When workers activate the
press, rubber bladders

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.640
inflate pressing the
wood into a ski shape.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:03:54.360
35 minutes later, the skis
come out of the press.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:56.385
A worker separates
them with a band saw.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.080
Using a jigsaw, they
trim the tip and tail.

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.750
The saw leaves a
rough edge so workers

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:12.705
sand the entire perimeter.

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.420
Then they taper and
bevel the sidewall.

00:04:23.420 --> 00:04:26.075
This gives the ski's a
better edge grip on snow.

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:32.520
The final step is
to peel off the film

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:34.140
that has been
protecting the nylon

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.370
top throughout construction.

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.910
These handmade skis come in
several different styles,

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:52.910
shapes, and sizes.

00:04:54.340 --> 00:04:57.160
All they need now
are bindings, then

00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.030
it's all downhill from here.

